description:
src/worker/mod.rs resolve_adapter() (line ~2812) silently falls back to the claude-sonnet built-in when the configured adapter (config.agent.default) is not found in the dispatcher's loaded adapter map. This causes workers to dispatch beads with claude-sonnet — which uses 'unbuffer -p claude' and triggers the PTY escape-sequence transform failure — instead of failing with a clear error.

## Root cause

When a worker starts with --agent claude-code-glm-47, if the adapter file is not found or not loaded into the dispatcher, resolve_adapter falls through to:

  if let Some(adapter) = self.dispatcher.adapter("claude-sonnet") {
      tracing::warn!(..., "configured adapter not found, using fallback");
      return Ok(adapter.clone());
  }

The warn log is never surfaced to the user and the worker silently degrades.

## Fix

Remove the claude-sonnet fallback block (lines ~2819-2826). The function should be:

  fn resolve_adapter(&self) -> Result<crate::dispatch::AgentAdapter> {
      let adapter_name = &self.config.agent.default;
      self.dispatcher.adapter(adapter_name)
          .cloned()
          .ok_or_else(|| anyhow::anyhow!(
              "configured agent adapter '{}' not found — check ~/.needle/agents/{}.yaml or ~/.local/bin/claude-config/agents/{}/config.json exists",
              adapter_name, adapter_name, adapter_name
          ))
  }

The last-resort first-built-in block should also be removed (or kept only if the adapter_name is literally empty/unset, which config validation should prevent).

## Complete when
- resolve_adapter returns Err (not Ok(claude-sonnet)) when config.agent.default is not in dispatcher
- Existing test resolve_adapter_returns_builtin updated to expect Err when adapter not found
- cargo test passes (push to main and wait for needle-ci)

Part of plan.md Phase 5 / ADR-001 (docs/adr/001-explore-strand-hardening.md). This is a PROPOSED AMENDMENT to ADR-001 Decision point 2, not a bug in bf-1hm79's implementation of what was asked — bf-1hm79 correctly shipped `hash(qualified_id) % N`, but the ADR's own de-herd goal has a gap the ratified design didn't anticipate.

## Evidence (2026-07-27, live on lab 100.81.129.38)

SEAM (workspace index 9 of 37 that day) had 2 real, unblocked, non-label-excluded ready beads that sat completely unclaimed for 4+ days despite an active ~10-worker fleet. Root cause, confirmed via `grep 'worker scan rotation' ~/.needle/logs/needle-*.stderr.log` across every live worker's full session history: `compute_start_index()` is `DefaultHasher(qualified_id) % n` — deterministic and **static for the life of a worker session**, because `qualified_id` never changes after construction. Sampled workers across 1.5-19h of their own session lifetime showed the *identical* start_index on every single cycle:
- `h9-roam` (index 2): every cycle landed on `gantry-rs`, unchanged for 1.5h+.
- `h5-drawrace` (index 4): every cycle landed on `mta-my-way`, unchanged for 1.5h+.
- `h8-roam`, `h3-cgraph`, `h10-roam` (all index 28, coincidentally): every cycle landed on `ai-code-battle` (empty) then `ARMOR` (candidates present but excluded — the still-live stale-Assignee bug), then stopped there. Three separate workers permanently dead-ended on the same repo.

bf-1hm79's own acceptance criterion — "two workers with different qualified ids visit workspaces in different rotations covering the full list" — only tests cross-worker variation at a point in time. It never tests same-worker variation across cycles, which is exactly where this breaks: de-herding workers *from each other* at boot doesn't guarantee any given workspace is ever reachable by *any* worker over that worker's whole session, if that worker's fixed hash happens to land near a workspace that's always non-empty (or always excluded, per the ARMOR case).

## Requested fix (operator-specified, this session)

1. **Shuffle the scan order at the start of every `evaluate()` invocation** — not derived from a fixed per-worker hash, not cached. A fresh `Vec::shuffle` (e.g. `rand::seq::SliceRandom`, already a Cargo.toml dependency) each cycle means every workspace gets a turn going first across enough cycles, for every worker, independent of directory-listing order or worker identity. This supersedes `compute_start_index`/`rotated_workspace_order` from bf-1hm79 — de-herding still happens (workers are very unlikely to shuffle into the same order at the same moment), it just isn't pinned to a static identity-derived value anymore.
2. **Refresh (re-discover) the workspace list at the start of every `evaluate()` invocation, unconditionally** — this changes bf-3peh4 (P5.7)'s scope from "every N cycles (config, default modest)" to "every cycle, no throttle knob." A plain `fs::read_dir` + `.beads/` check over ~40 entries is cheap; ADR-001's "Consequences" section already anticipated bounding this cost via mtime pre-checks if it ever matters, but even without that optimization the raw cost is negligible at current fleet scale. **Please coordinate with whoever picks up bf-3peh4 — this bead and that one are the same underlying change (rediscovery throttle removal) and should not be implemented twice.**
3. Verified already, no action needed: there is **no hardcoded workspace list in any default configuration path** — `Config::default()`, `~/NEEDLE`'s own `.needle.yaml`, the `needle init` embedded template (`src/cli/mod.rs`), and lab's live `~/.config/needle/config.yaml` all already ship `explore.workspaces: []`. This was apparently already fixed following the 2026-07-19 incident referenced in the `explore.rs` module doc comment. Just confirming the constraint holds — not a task item.

## Reference implementation (already written and validated, NOT currently on disk)

A full hand-written implementation of points 1 and 2 was completed and confirmed via `cargo check --lib` (clean compile) against the pre-bf-5i02o/pre-bf-2vyaw version of `src/strand/explore.rs` — i.e. **before** `Filters.exclude_ids` / the `evaluate(&self, _store: &dyn BeadStore, _exclusions: &HashSet<BeadId>)` signature change landed. It was lost from the shared working tree via an unrelated `git pull --rebase` collision (uncommitted changes swept into another worker's commit, then reconciled away during the rebase) before it could be committed — nothing was pushed, so this is the only record of it. Whoever picks this up will need to re-derive it against the current `evaluate()` signature (with the exclusions parameter) rather than apply it verbatim, but the design is straightforward:

```rust
// New import:
use rand::seq::SliceRandom;

// Remove: compute_start_index(), rotated_workspace_order(), the
// cycles_since_rediscovery AtomicU32 field, and the rediscovery_cycles
// gating field/checks. Replace with:

fn shuffled_workspace_order(&self) -> Vec<PathBuf> {
    let mut shuffled = { self.workspaces.lock().unwrap().clone() };
    shuffled.shuffle(&mut rand::thread_rng());
    shuffled
}

// rediscover_workspaces(): keep the auto_discovery_mode pinned-mode guard,
// drop the "if self.rediscovery_cycles == 0 { skip }" check entirely.

// In evaluate(), replace the AtomicU32 fetch_add/threshold block with an
// unconditional call:
let added = self.rediscover_workspaces();
if added > 0 { /* existing log */ }
// ...and replace `self.rotated_workspace_order()` / `self.compute_start_index()`
// with `self.shuffled_workspace_order()` for the scan-order source.
```

Test suite changes needed: bf-1hm79's own tests (the ones asserting deterministic start-index-per-qualified-id, that a rotation starts at the computed index, that different workers get different rotations, and that the hash distribution is reasonable — see the "Rotation Tests" section of `src/strand/explore.rs`) assert determinism/hash-based positioning and will need replacing with tests asserting: (a) full coverage with no duplicates per shuffle call, (b) repeated calls on the *same* strand instance produce varied orders (proves it's not cached), (c) reasonable first-position distribution across many calls (proves no structural bias toward directory order). Same idea for bf-3peh4's rediscovery tests — replace "N cycles needed before rediscovery" assertions with "the very next cycle already picked it up."

## Acceptance

- `shuffled_workspace_order()` (or equivalent) returns a fresh Fisher-Yates-shuffled order on every call — unit test proving repeated calls on one strand instance produce more than one distinct order.
- Workspace re-discovery runs unconditionally every `evaluate()` cycle in auto-discovery mode (no throttle config needed) — unit test proving a workspace created between two `evaluate()` calls is picked up on the very next one, not after N cycles.
- Existing pinned-mode tests (re-discovery skipped when `config.workspaces` is non-empty) still pass.
- Coordinate with bf-2vyaw (exclude_ids/exclusions plumbing) and bf-3peh4 (periodic re-discovery) — this bead's scope overlaps both; don't duplicate work, do a `git pull` and read current `evaluate()` before starting.
